A shape the *program* declares, composed out of the stdlib's. What this
records is that such a declaration never reaches passCanvas at all: the
`Chip` and `Panel` NodeInsts are gone before `emitShape` is asked anything,
so the three brackets below are the three `rect`s' own -- each written in
`shapes.rect[language]`, which saves, applies its style and restores.

That is worth pinning because it is not where one would look for it.
`isPrimitiveComponent` exempts a segmented tree's members from passInlinePure,
so a reader reasonably expects a user shape to survive to passCanvas and be
bracketed there by the composed-shape branch, which would nest a bracket
inside a bracket. It does not, because passNoInlineComponents composes it away
first -- every platform sets `InlineComponents=false`, and that pass asks
nothing about trees.

So the exemption buys a user shape nothing, and removing it would not change
this file. A `Panel` holding two `Chip`s is here rather than one shape alone
so that a nesting, if one ever appeared, would be two deep and unmistakable.

deny out/none/html/index.html `(?m)^\s*ctx\.save\(\);\n\s*ctx\.save\(\);` -- a composed shape bracketed on top of its leaves would nest two saves; each rect brackets itself and nothing wraps that
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `(?m)^\s*ctx\.Save\(\)\n\s*ctx\.Save\(\)` -- the same claim on the other target

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import draw "sngl:ui/draw"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
}

component Chip(x = 0.0, y = 0.0) draw.shape {
    draw.rect(x=x, y=y, w=4.0, h=4.0, style=draw.CanvasStyle{fill=color{r=255, a=255}})
}

component Panel() draw.shape {
    Chip(x=0.0, y=0.0)
    Chip(x=8.0, y=0.0)
}

component main() ui.node {
    draw.canvas(width=40px, height=20px) {
        Panel()
        draw.rect(x=0.0, y=10.0, w=4.0, h=4.0, style=draw.CanvasStyle{fill=color{b=255, a=255}})
    }
}

ui.window {
    main()
}
